/T (Dr. Alessandra Gregori is a prominent Italian specialist in the authentication, attribution, and valuation of Old Master paintings, with particular expertise in works by Caravaggio and the complex artistic production associated with his international circle. She is the niece of the legendary Professor Mina Gregori one of the most respected and foundational authorities in modern Caravaggio studies. Carrying forward this distinguished scholarly lineage, Dr. Gregori continues a rigorous tradition of traditional connoisseurship, archival analysis, and deep historical research into Italian Baroque painting. Her work routinely bridges the gap between stylistic analysis, historical context, and the fine-grained questions of disputed authorship.)
/E (Dr. Alessandra Gregori is a prominent Italian specialist in the authentication, attribution, and valuation of Old Master paintings, with particular expertise in works by Caravaggio and the complex artistic production associated with his international circle. She is the niece of the legendary Professor Mina Gregori one of the most respected and foundational authorities in modern Caravaggio studies. Carrying forward this distinguished scholarly lineage, Dr. Gregori continues a rigorous tradition of traditional connoisseurship, archival analysis, and deep historical research into Italian Baroque painting. Her work routinely bridges the gap between stylistic analysis, historical context, and the fine-grained questions of disputed authorship.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
694 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 335 0 R
/T (Her professional activity focuses on connoisseurship, forensic provenance research, and the evaluation of complex, high-stakes attribution cases. By combining exact art-historical analysis with acute practical market expertise, she provides comprehensive evaluations that satisfy both academic standards and the commercial art world. In the Archimedes case, Dr. Gregori provided a critical, independent art-historical assessment and structural valuation of the canvas. Furthermore, she served as the scientific moderator for the Monaco Symposium, playing a pivotal role in publically presenting the painting and orchestrating its examination within an international framework of leading consensus scholars.)
/E (Her professional activity focuses on connoisseurship, forensic provenance research, and the evaluation of complex, high-stakes attribution cases. By combining exact art-historical analysis with acute practical market expertise, she provides comprehensive evaluations that satisfy both academic standards and the commercial art world. In the Archimedes case, Dr. Gregori provided a critical, independent art-historical assessment and structural valuation of the canvas. Furthermore, she served as the scientific moderator for the Monaco Symposium, playing a pivotal role in publically presenting the painting and orchestrating its examination within an international framework of leading consensus scholars.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [2]
