/P 342 0 R
/T (After a long day of presentations in Monaco, Archimedes was finally unveiled. My immediate impression was that the painting possessed precisely that quality of presence. It commanded attention.)
/E (After a long day of presentations in Monaco, Archimedes was finally unveiled. My immediate impression was that the painting possessed precisely that quality of presence. It commanded attention.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
729 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 342 0 R
/T (Later I sat below the painting, slightly to the right. From that position the image seemed to acquire another dimension of meaning, much as many of Caravaggio's works reveal new aspects of their narrative when viewed from an oblique angle. I am reminded of the Denial of Saint Peter in New York and the Cardsharps at the Kimbell Art Museum in Fort Worth, to name but two examples where Caravaggio's storytelling appears to unfold differently as the viewer changes position.)
/E (Later I sat below the painting, slightly to the right. From that position the image seemed to acquire another dimension of meaning, much as many of Caravaggio's works reveal new aspects of their narrative when viewed from an oblique angle. I am reminded of the Denial of Saint Peter in New York and the Cardsharps at the Kimbell Art Museum in Fort Worth, to name but two examples where Caravaggio's storytelling appears to unfold differently as the viewer changes position.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
730 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 342 0 R
/T (Wanting to test whether this impression was merely subjective, I invited Dr Katarzyna Papiez a medical surgeon with no particular involvement in art history to view the painting from the same position. To my surprise, she immediately perceived something similar. Unfortunately, the painting was soon whisked away, and with it the opportunity to explore this observation further and see whether it resonated with other viewers. Whether these impressions can ever be measured is another matter. What I can say is that the painting possesses undeniable power and considerable potential. Some works invite analysis; others invite experience. Archimedes, in my view, does both.)
/E (Wanting to test whether this impression was merely subjective, I invited Dr Katarzyna Papiez a medical surgeon with no particular involvement in art history to view the painting from the same position. To my surprise, she immediately perceived something similar. Unfortunately, the painting was soon whisked away, and with it the opportunity to explore this observation further and see whether it resonated with other viewers. Whether these impressions can ever be measured is another matter. What I can say is that the painting possesses undeniable power and considerable potential. Some works invite analysis; others invite experience. Archimedes, in my view, does both.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [3]
>>
